# Agent memory, second batch: the cases that take memory_write and recall to 50 (70 cases).
#
# Only the new cases live here; the original 46 stay in `memory.jsonl` so the run of
# 2026-09-24 and its recording stay reproducible as published. memory_collision is not
# extended here: it has four labels rather than two, its `duplicate` branch never fired at the
# shipped thresholds, and a batch that only adds cases would not settle that. It gets its own
# treatment once the binary points have their precision targets.
#
# memory_write is deliberately harder than the first batch, which scored 16/16. The whole
# difficulty sits in one distinction, because it is the one that turns a memory store into a
# slow copy of the repository:
#
#   store: something the agent learned by doing, that it cannot get back by re-reading a
#          source it already holds. Operational constraints, the reason behind a decision,
#          a negative result and why, a standing instruction from the client.
#   skip:  anything re-derivable. A literal quotation from a document already in the volume,
#          a fact about code the agent can grep, restatements of the task, progress narration,
#          transient state, and general world knowledge.
#
# The trap cases are facts that are true, durable, specific and useless to store, because the
# source is already in hand (mw-25, mw-31, mw-37, mw-43). The mirror traps are facts that
# sound like chatter but encode a constraint that cost real work to learn (mw-18, mw-30,
# mw-40).
#
# recall is the same idea pointed the other way. The first batch was 14/14 with obvious cases;
# these turn on the two hard families the point actually faces:
#
#   look: a turn that reads as self-contained but silently depends on something agreed
#         earlier - a format, a preference, an exclusion, a decision already taken.
#   skip: a turn that sounds like it needs history but carries its own context inline, and
#         pure transformation of text the user just supplied.
#
# rc-39 is a declared negative control: it turns on comparing two dates (paper Section 5.3).
#
# Annotator 1 (author) labelled every case before any decision was run; Annotator 2 is a
# separate generative model, blind to that label and to the evaluator's answer. Agreement is
# reported in the results directory. Two annotators, one of them a model, is not the two
# independent humans the literature assumes, and the paper says so where it uses these.
